## No tree, and no ceiling

`kotoba-lang/pattern` gains `kotoba/pattern_emit.kotoba`: the regex-subset
compiler emitting the string program `pattern-vm` reads, building no tree.

Parse and emit are fused. Each function answers the code it emitted AS THE
STRING plus the source index it stopped at, and a quantifier does not need its
operand as a value -- it needs it EMITTED AGAIN at another program counter --
so it remembers the operand's SOURCE SPAN and re-runs the emitter over it.
`a{3}` compiles the same three characters three times, which costs scanning
and buys the absence of the tree, which is exactly the shape a `:document`
(depth 8) cannot hold.

Measured over the same corpus, comparing the two compilers THROUGH THE MACHINE
rather than by bytes -- the `.cljc` shares a class table between identical
classes and this one appends ranges where it meets them, so two correct
programs differ in bytes, and what has to agree is what they ACCEPT:

  agreed through pattern-vm 168   both refused 16   over a document bound 0
  disagreements 0

against 140 agreed and 43 over-bound for the document-program compiler.

## The first consumer

`kotoba-lang/kiyaku` `kotoba/postal_core.kotoba` holds the six postal formats
`kiyaku.account` spells as regex literals -- JP, US, GB, CA, DE, FR -- as
PROGRAMS compiled from those same regexes by `pattern-emit`. It is the first
thing in this workspace to use `kotoba-lang/pattern` instead of hand-rolling a
scanner, and twelve modules hand-roll one today.

`test/kotoba/postal_parity.cljs` puts the table module and the matcher
together and checks the answers against the namespace that ships: 35/35 agree,
including a double space in a UK code, lower case where the format demands
upper, and every length one off. Pointing the JP entry at the DE program turns
exactly the JP cases red.

## What that consumer could not do, and why

A MODULE THAT DECLARES `:schemas` CANNOT BE REQUIRED BY ANOTHER MODULE.
Measured and minimised to four files (kotoba-lang/kotoba-sema#78): requiring a
schema-free module links, requiring a schema-declaring one is refused with
\"only one :export, one :capabilities and alias-only :require clauses are
admitted\" -- a message about the REQUIRING module's ns clauses, which are
identical in both cases.

Records are how a Kotoba function carries more than five arguments
(`:max-parameters 5`), so most libraries that do real work declare `:schemas`.
`pattern-vm` and `pattern-emit` are records throughout, so nothing can require
them today. The consumer therefore keeps what it can own -- WHICH program a
country uses -- as a schema-free module, and the host loads both artifacts.
That works, and it is not composition.

## Four bugs, all of which read as plausible answers

  * Re-emitting an operand's span looked at the character AT the span's end --
    the quantifier itself -- and treated it as a quantifier again, so the
    emitter recursed forever. `a*`, `a+`, `a?`, `a{1,3}`, `a{2,}` all answered
    \"Maximum call stack size exceeded\"; a bare `a` was fine.
  * `{n}` took its hi from a field the answer-builder fills with a STRING, and
    the i64 reader reads a string as 0, so `a{3}` was `a{3,0}` and refused
    itself with :count/backwards.
  * Five tests were defined and not in `:export`, so the self-check reported
    \"7/7 passed\" while twelve existed. The runner reads the export vector.
  * A commit message written with `-m \"…`backticks`…\"` lost two words to
    command substitution. CLAUDE.md warns about that shape inside heredocs;
    it is the same hazard one quoting level up.

## And one lost afternoon

The first version of the emitter -- 560 lines, compiling but answering
\"internal compiler error\" -- lived UNTRACKED in a worktree, and the worktree
was removed under the session. An untracked file looks clean to a retirement
sweep. CLAUDE.md already says WIP belongs in a commit on a session branch;
the rewrite lands as a stub first and grows in commits, and that is now how
this session works.

The rewrite also answered the question the lost version could not: with the
quantifier machinery removed, slice 1 compiled and ran, so the internal error
was in the second cycle through the emitter and nowhere else.
